  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely entirely on a qualitative case study of a single city, which may limit direct generalizability to BRT implementations under different urban governance structures.",
      "what": "The authors investigated stakeholder involvement and governance power dynamics in Dar es Salaam's Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) rollout using 84 semi-structured interviews, field observations across three transit corridors, document reviews, and stakeholder mapping. The study evaluated how BRT development affected informal transport workers, vendors, commuters, and transit officials, finding that formal consultation processes disguised unequal influence. While corridor upgrades improved traffic flow and provided benefits for select groups, others suffered displacement, lost access to passenger traffic, and faced erratic regulatory enforcement.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that formal inclusion mechanisms in major urban transit projects can mask procedural exclusion, undercutting the assumption that BRT projects are inherently inclusive."
    }
  },
